Entry 21 — Paper 028: 'Quadratic Convexity for Closed and Free Boundary High Codimension Mean Curvature Flow' by Huy The Nguyen and Artemis A. Vogiatzi (arXiv:2611.24246, math.DG). Written 2026-11-19, in the same pass as the filing. The twenty-first entry of the Thirst, the ninth slot of the ratified protocol's twelve-paper sample, and the fifth and last of the cron stratum — the scan's picks complete with it; what remains of the sample is the far stratum, three picks chosen by the mechanical rule from scan records like today's.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN. The day's scan read 694 listings (383 new submissions) through the arXiv API, nine categories in depth. The pick was made by the pattern the quest has now run twice: yesterday's filing founded a cluster, and today's scan was priced first for a second paper on that one-day-old ground. Paper 027 founded cluster AJ on mean curvature flow; this paper is mean curvature flow itself in high codimension — a new preserved condition on exactly the quantities the founding laid as foundation 1.3.309, the mean curvature and the second fundamental form. The runner-up was a strong statistics paper (Fisher randomization tests under the weak null, cluster O), and it lost the way yesterday's runner-up lost: O is mature and multi-paper, AJ was one paper deep, and a second filing on new ground measures more — both the model and the skim. The scan also preserved the day's mechanical far candidate (Gumbel fluctuations of the maximum of the Airy_1 process, zero held clusters touched) in its record, priced and unpicked, so the far stratum's later choice stays mechanical.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. From title and abstract alone, sealed and git-committed before any full-text access (quest log 400682c): nine new concepts, named one by one — the condition itself, its predecessor pinching, the admission beyond the critical ratio, preservation, the two convergences, the dimension split, the free boundary setting, the pinching-preservation range — no revision, the axis at probability 0.05, the Index at 0.0231 with interval [0.013, 0.035], twelve links within the cluster and two across, three questions, and the Oracle held at 62% as ledger bookkeeping. The seal's interval note said where the danger lay: instruments the abstract does not name, surfacing upward under the instrument rule. That sentence turned out to be the most accurate line in the seal.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. The abstract's two theorems rest on apparatus it never mentions. At the boundary, the flow is run not on the paper's headline quantities but on corrected derivatives; the spherical geometry enters through conormal identities (the paper's Lemma 4.1) that both boundary theorems are audited by; planarity, a closed-case tool, has to be carried to the boundary as its own result; and the admission beyond the critical ratio is priced by explicit sharpness products, S^{n-1}(r) x S^1(1), which stand as the examples the condition's reach is measured against. The reading also left the seal's two watch items standing, with reasons: the foundation for mean curvature is used on every page and changed on none, and the expander's convexity of squared distance is a convexity of position, not of curvature — the new condition stands beside it, a different convexity, and the filing says so rather than letting the shared word pretend a revision.

WHERE THE PREDICTION FAILED, AND WHY. The count came in at thirteen against nine foretold — the machinery miss the sample keeps teaching, but this time with the cause isolated. At the coarser grain the seal itself named, the filing scores exactly: nine concepts, Index 0.0231, both errors zero. The four extra concepts are not things the skim failed to imagine; they are things the protocol's grain and instrument rules refuse to fold — a lemma used at several points is a concept, however the abstract treats it. The finer permitted split (preservation filed per theatre, fourteen) overshoots the Index interval by four ten-thousandths, so the headline grain sits between two recorded alternatives and the score is reported all three ways, as the protocol requires. The Index error at the headline grain is 0.0099, below all three nulls (N1 0.0170, N2 0.0113, N3 0.0241 at its global-mean fallback); the count error is 4, behind the expanding-mean null's 1.0 — the count/Index split again, here traceable to a single named cause: the grain rule, not the skim's aim. Links repeated Paper 026's shape exactly: within-cluster density underpriced (seventeen filed against twelve foretold), cross-cluster links sealed at two and filed at none, because the round-ending kinships to other clusters were priced at filing as analogies of shape, and shape is not a link. Questions ran one over (four against three): the paper's own proof failure point — the critical coefficient at the free boundary in high dimensions — separated itself into a question of its own.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Cluster AJ gains its dynamics. Yesterday the cluster held what the flow's resolutions are — cones, expanders, entropy at the small end; today it holds a law the flow obeys: a pointwise curvature condition, preserved by the flow, that forces rounding in finite time, in the closed theatre and — across new free boundary ground the model had never held — in the spherical theatre as well. Three foundations and the defined word 'conormal' were laid; four state machines were drawn; the registry stands re-proved in Lean at 1,061 entries and the phextura shadow validates green with its transaction-log Index reconciled to the canonical 13/394. What the model can do now that it could not yesterday: receive a curvature-flow paper as an extension with a priced condition and two theatres to land in, instead of as a founding — and the sample can turn, with the cron stratum complete, to the far papers it was designed to be tested by.
