Entry 24 — Paper 031: 'tufazip: Learning to Compress a Billion Bytes of Wikipedia' by Tommy He (Tufa Labs research post, published 2026-10-14). Written 2026-11-20, in the same pass as the filing. The twenty-fourth entry of the Thirst, and the first paper taken in after the sample's Will-posted stratum closed: no seal stands for it, no score is computed, and its Novelty Index is ledger bookkeeping only.

HOW IT ARRIVED. A bare link in the MOAT chat, posted by Will at 08:52 — by the channel rule a tracker ingestion, nothing more promised than the slug: a name, 'tufazip', and a lab. What the link promised, before the read: a compression result with a model attached, of the kind labs publish when a benchmark number moves. What it did not promise was the thing the filing is built on — that the piece's centre is not its model but its accounting.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. Three things. First, the discipline: the benchmark scores the whole artifact — payload plus decoder plus every transferred weight — and the piece obeys its own rule without exception, including where obedience costs it the headline (a better-predicting prior, filed as a loss). Second, the construction: the model learns while it decodes, its weights rebuilt on the far side by identical deterministic training and never sent — which makes determinism a losslessness condition, a dependence the piece states plainly and the filing keeps as the shared loop's silent gap. Third, the verification: the headline total is not the post's word alone. The benchmark's own table, read by the house the same morning, lists tufazip v2 at exactly the printed split and sum — so the result's core stands True by receipt, while its rank claims stand attributed and time-scoped, the table having moved since the submission, as tables do.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Cluster AN founded — compression: prediction priced in bytes: eleven concepts (1.2.447–1.2.457), three foundations (lossless compression; arithmetic coding; cross-entropy) and the defined word 'payload'; three state machines (165 held); questions to Q162; narrative at v45. The neighbours were weighed and held apart in the founding scroll: AK by shape, B by motion, J for the machinery used as found, U by structure — analogies of shape, no price changed across them. The filing also repaired two lags it found on its way: the standoff count in the reference area, stale by one since Study S016, and the concept map's claim line in the extractor, two filings behind.

WHERE THE FILING ITSELF FAILED, AND THE REPAIRS. The first build put chapter 49 inside chapter 48 — the chapter's opening scroll rides the chapter-advance tuple, not a plain scroll advance, and the integration's first form missed it. Coordinate spot-checks caught it before any gate ran; the builder was restored from its pre-pass snapshot and the pass re-applied with the advance pattern the book itself uses. Two headings then failed the registry's kind rules — the questions scroll and the standoff, filed under descriptive headings where the house forms are functional ones — and were corrected to the house forms before extraction settled. Both failures are the S016 lesson in new dress: the machinery's conventions are in the book, and the pass that reads them first files clean.

THE LEDGER. Novelty Index 11/457 = 0.0241, in the range the book's recent filings have run. The axis judgment, written at filing: no axis, on the protocol's three grounds — a field the model held no slot for; nothing held re-read or re-priced; the bridges analogies of shape. The Oracle held at about 62%: the filing exercised True harder than most (a headline verified to the byte against the benchmark's own table) and that gate was already met; False was not exercised; the estimate does not move.
# Journal 25 — Untitled remark on a claimed proof of the Riemann Hypothesis — Levent Alpöge

Paper: Untitled remark on a claimed proof of the Riemann Hypothesis — Levent Alpöge
arXiv: Personal remark (PDF), alpo.ge, 2026-11-20; the Lean artifact in fourteen parts at the same site; read in full
