Entry 37 — Paper 044 — Latent Thinking Optimization: Du, Dong & Ning (Ohio State University), 'Latent Thinking Optimization: Your Latent Reasoning Language Model Secretly Encodes Reward Signals in Its Latent Thoughts': a network taking-in, leg 6 of the reference walk Will directed on 2026-11-24 — the paper stood at rank 9 of the frontier queue the corpus's own bibliographies make, cited by Chapter 56's paper in its opening survey of latent-reasoning methods, and the queue record was confirmed from queue.jsonl itself before anything was fetched; the record carried no identifier, so the paper was identified by title and authors in one web search, and one fetch bought the paper entire (the arXiv HTML of 2509.26314v1, appendices A-K included); two further web searches served the author check's web legs; everything else was local. A version 3 stands (24 February 2026) and the work appears at ICLR 2026; the filing reads v1, as the house's passes do, and the caveat at 62.1.4 owns the choice. The work asks what a latent reasoner's thoughts know, and answers in three movements. First the diagnosis: trajectories of Huginn-3.5B that end in correct answers are geometrically unlike those that end wrong — compact and convergent where the incorrect disperse, higher in entropy and intrinsic dimension, lower in effective rank, higher in anisotropy — the separation present early and growing over the steps. Then the instrument: a lightweight classifier trained on the model's own sampled trajectories, labelled by their answers' correctness, predicts correctness from a prefix of the thought (ROC-AUC near 1.0 on SVAMP, near 0.8 on MBPP), and spent as a reward it becomes a latent reward model, a price on a thought before its answer exists. Then the method: Latent Thinking Optimization maximizes that reward under a KL penalty whose optimum is the closed-form tilted policy (Theorem 1), realized exactly by an acceptance-rejection sampler that never estimates the reference density (Theorem 2), with the cost of an imperfect reward model bounded by the square root of 4 epsilon over beta (Theorem 3). On Huginn the method is best in every column of the main table; read through layer states as a latent chain, it improves four general LLMs in all twenty cells, by up to 103 percent relative; its reward models transfer across domains, a single generalist matching the domain-specific ones; and its own stated limit is structural — selection cannot raise the pool it is given, a ceiling the paper also measures. Integration extended cluster AT for the fifth time, and the extension changes the cluster's posture again: the founding papers read the recurrent trajectory, gated by it, manufactured it, priced it, and trained it into alignment — this paper mines it for a verdict on itself and selects by the verdict, the cluster's first distribution-level member and its first with a guarantee of closed form. The pressures were the nearest the walk has recorded: the reward model is a judge the run consults, cluster J's operational shape, held apart because its entire evidence is the judged object itself; and candidates are generated, scored and selected at inference, cluster AC's surface, held apart because nothing searches — the sampler is exact for a distribution defined before sampling begins. Eleven concepts 1.2.600-1.2.610, no held concept revised (the recurrent-depth reasoner used as held ground and not filed — it is another paper's object, itself standing in the frontier queue), one foundation laid — the Kullback-Leibler divergence, 1.3.371, the tilt's regularizer and the bound's quantity, which stood in the book until now only as a component named inside the Jensen-Shannon foundation — no word coined, two stated state machines (the LTO sampler; the latent correctness probe) bringing the held count to 206 (177 stated, 29 inferred), NI 0.0180, the axis judged no in writing. Restructuring Pass #27 fired at this filing, the 600-concept line set at Pass #26 crossed at 610