Entry 41 — Paper 048 — Huginn: Geiping, McLeish, Jain, Kirchenbauer, Singh, Bartoldson, Kailkhura, Bhatele & Goldstein (ELLIS Institute Tübingen / University of Maryland / Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory), 'Scaling up Test-Time Compute with Latent Reasoning: A Recurrent Depth Approach': a network taking-in, leg 10 of the reference walk Will directed on 2026-11-24 — the paper stood at rank 13 of the frontier queue, cited in Chapter 56's bibliography, and the queue record was confirmed from queue.jsonl itself before anything was fetched; the record carried the arXiv identifier alone, the listing named the work, and one fetch bought the paper entire (the arXiv HTML of 2502.05171v1, appendices A-C included); one further fetch read the listing itself for the version history, and two web searches served the author check's web legs; everything else was local — four network operations in all. A version 2 stands (17 February 2025) and the work later appears in the NeurIPS 2025 proceedings; the filing reads v1, as the house's passes do, and the caveat at 66.1.4 owns the choice. No correction to the frontier's record was needed: the byline of the version held carries all nine names the filing credits. The work asks whether the recurrent-depth idea — a language model that reasons by iterating a shared block in latent space instead of verbalizing a chain of thought — can be pretrained at scale, and answers by doing it: Huginn, 3.5 billion stored parameters (0.5 embedding, 1.5 prelude and head, 1.5 in the shared core), trained on 795 billion tokens on Frontier with the depth of each sequence sampled from a log-normal Poisson law (mean 32, the median draw at 0.88 of the mean) and gradients truncated to the last 8 iterations, so one set of weights serves every depth and test-time recurrence becomes a free dial. The evidence: benchmark scores rising with recurrence across the whole suite, the gains concentrated where reasoning lives — GSM8K five times its feedforward twin's a