Entry 42 — Paper 049 — Reasoning Beyond Language: Chen, Zhao, Xia, Lu, Wang, Chen, Zhang, Wang, Li & Shen (The Hong Kong Polytechnic University / Eastern Institute of Technology, Ningbo), 'Reasoning Beyond Language: A Comprehensive Survey on Latent Chain-of-Thought Reasoning': a network taking-in, leg 11 of the reference walk Will directed on 2026-11-24 — the paper stood at rank 14 of the frontier queue, cited in Chapter 56's bibliography, and the queue record was confirmed from queue.jsonl itself before anything was fetched; the record carried the identifier, the title and the full byline, and the held text's byline agrees with it exactly, all ten names in order; one fetch read the arXiv listing, one fetch bought the paper entire (the arXiv HTML of 2505.16782v1, Sections 1-8, limitations, ethics statement and reference list), and two web searches served the author check's web legs; everything else was local — four network operations in all. Versions 2 (1 November 2025) and 3 stand on the listing; the filing reads v1, as the house's passes do — the v1 abstract states the four-perspective taxonomy the body carries, where the current abstract compresses it to the horizontal–vertical axis, and the note at 67.1.4 owns the difference. No correction to the frontier's record was needed. The work is a survey — the first, its authors say, for latent chain-of-thought specifically — and it asks its field to see itself whole: reasoning decoupled from verbalized language, sorted from four perspectives (token-wise strategies; internal mechanisms; analysis and interpretability; applications) and cross-cut by the horizontal axis of the sequence and the vertical axis of depth. Its contents: the discrete markers that scaffold computation, whose structure, on Pfau et al.'s evidence, matters more than their content; the continuous tokens a model feeds itself or receives from an auxiliary module (Coconut, CCoT, CODI, SoftCoT); the mechanisms — computation bought with recurrence, or chai